    Migrate from Apache HttpClient 4 to HttpClient 5 (#8166)
    
    Apache HttpClient 4.x and HttpCore 4.x are end of life and will not
    receive security fixes anymore. This replaces them with HttpClient 5.6
    and HttpCore 5.4 throughout Opencast and removes the private copy of
    HttpClient 5 the OpenSearch client module embedded so far.
    
    Packaging:
    
    - The 5.x jars are no OSGi bundles, hence they are wrapped in the Karaf
    feature with versioned exports and optional imports for the optional
    dependencies of HttpClient (conscrypt, compression libraries,
    junixsocket).
    - Modules declare httpclient5/httpcore5 directly; modules which only got
    commons-codec transitively via the old bundle declare it now.
    
    Trusted HTTP client:
    
    - `TrustedHttpClient` uses `ClassicHttpRequest` and
    `ClassicHttpResponse` and gained a handler based `execute` which closes
    the response automatically.
    - The implementation uses a single pooled client instead of creating a
    client per request. The pool size can be configured with
    `org.opencastproject.security.http.max.connections.per.route` and
    `org.opencastproject.security.http.max.connections`.
    - The manual digest handshake for requests with a body is built on the
    5.x challenge parser and now sends `qop`, `nc` and `cnonce`.
    - The nonce retry used to compare the reason phrase against a text
    Spring Security never sends and was therefore dead code. It now triggers
    on a challenge marked `stale="true"` and keeps its configuration
    options.
    - `HttpResponseWrapper` is gone; it only existed to key responses in a
    map of clients.
    
    Besides the mechanical API changes in all modules, a number of HTTP
    clients and responses which were never closed are closed now.
    
    
    ### How to test this patch
    
    Since this is litterally everywhere in Opencast, to test this, just use
    Opencast for a bit :D
    
